<feed xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2005/Atom"><title>Breaking news from AFRICA, Maghreb and Sub-Saharan Africa - France 24</title><subtitle type="html">FRANCE 24’s coverage of events, politics and social issues across Africa, including North Africa (the Maghreb) and the Sahel, aided by our network of correspondents and experts across the continent.</subtitle><id>https://www.france24.com/en/africa/rss</id><updated>2026-09-16T19:38:55Z</updated><author><name>rssync</name></author><link rel="self" href="rss" type="application/atom+xml" /><link rel="alternate" href="https://www.france24.com/en/africa/" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.france24.com/en/africa/rss" type="application/rss+xml" /><entry><title>WHO sees 'encouraging signs' in DR Congo Ebola response but says outbreak 'far from over'</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:582c8c6571f97c7dde9ffcef1ce74efc3229dd76ee8aa81f8472d94e5f20cbda</id><updated>2026-09-16T19:38:20Z</updated><published>2026-09-16T15:29:37Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../../pages/www.france24.com/en/africa/20260916-who-sees-encouraging-signs-in-dr-congo-ebola-response-but-says-outbreak-far-from-over--b7a7c127a44a.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="http://www.france24.com/en/africa/20260916-who-sees-encouraging-signs-in-dr-congo-ebola-response-but-says-outbreak-far-from-over" /><summary type="html">The World Health Organization said Wednesday that the fight against the Ebola outbreak in DR Congo was showing “encouraging signs" but that the epidemic was "far from over". More than 7,000 cases have been reported across seven provinces as of September 11.</summary><category term="Africa" /></entry><entry><title>At least 82 killed in gold mine collapse in remote area of Sudan, paramilitary RSF say</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:21c9a55478001d327f72af7de88022973b312118025d1a57a85ae80ad4491d7b</id><updated>2026-09-16T19:38:55Z</updated><published>2026-09-16T11:54:54Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../../pages/www.france24.com/en/africa/20260916-sudan-gold-mine-collapse-kills-at-least-67-people--0a2ff1999c68.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="http://www.france24.com/en/africa/20260916-sudan-gold-mine-collapse-kills-at-least-67-people" /><summary type="html">A gold mine collapse on Wednesday killed at least 82 people in a remote area in Sudan, a source in the paramilitary Rapid Support Forces reported. The destruction occurred at the al-Zara gold mine, located in West Kordofan province – an area under the control of the RSF, who have been at war with the country’s military for over three years.</summary><category term="Africa" /></entry><entry><title>Russian war, African recruits</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:7871f941af594ac877486db69c924fb1a01eed66879234fa5ce0824e0ba27c09</id><updated>2026-09-16T19:36:48Z</updated><published>2026-09-15T22:13:20Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../../pages/www.france24.com/en/russian-war-african-recruits--06e9911a8624.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="http://www.france24.com/en/russian-war-african-recruits" /><summary type="html">In this edition: thousands of Africans have found themselves in Ukraine fighting or working on behalf of the Kremlin. We speak to a journalist who just got back from Ukraine. Also, in the Democratic Republic of Congo, the C64 opposition coalition held a demonstration in the capital Kinshasa. And finally, Kenya's celebrating a stellar athletics victory as the country beats London in the race to host the 2029 World Championships.</summary><category term="Eye on Africa" /></entry><entry><title>Nigeria's Dangote refinery launches biggest African IPO</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:cdfd574e4132945b2d468e4704403abc7621617dca3861bf9026a7b57357ce42</id><updated>2026-09-16T19:36:48Z</updated><published>2026-09-15T00:05:32Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../../pages/www.france24.com/en/africa/20260915-nigeria-s-dangote-refinery-launches-biggest-african-ipo-for-retail-investors--d05fe90057ad.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="http://www.france24.com/en/africa/20260915-nigeria-s-dangote-refinery-launches-biggest-african-ipo-for-retail-investors" /><summary type="html">Africa’s richest man Aliko Dangote on Monday launched an IPO for his $19 billion Nigerian oil refinery, seeking $1.6 billion from retail investors across the continent. The offering values Africa’s largest refinery at about $49 billion and has drawn both strong investor interest and concerns over its high valuation.</summary><category term="Africa" /></entry><entry><title>Nigerian oil refinery Dangote aims to raise 1.6 billion dollars</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:ba85a0e4c281104bdd16faa00cd16907c577c898049d907c29d19f5ffb42e8a3</id><updated>2026-09-16T19:36:48Z</updated><published>2026-09-14T20:31:28Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../../pages/www.france24.com/en/nigerian-oil-refinery-dangote-aims-to-raise-1-6-billion-dollars--8328c51224d1.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="http://www.france24.com/en/nigerian-oil-refinery-dangote-aims-to-raise-1-6-billion-dollars" /><summary type="html">In tonight's edition: Nigeria's Dangote refinery opens itself to public ownership with plans to raise $1.6 billion dollars from retail investors across the continent. Also, DR Congo’s President Felix Tshisekedi calls on the National Service to clean up the streets of Kinshasa. And with a month and a half to go until the Youth Olympic Games begin in Senegal, the Olympic flame begins its tour of the host country.</summary><category term="Eye on Africa" /></entry><entry><title>Nigeria's Dangote refinery launches continent's biggest IPO</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:33c19efa2b113099f7d46370e2bacec9e9fa0b3773aac0d2304aa1f28760918d</id><updated>2026-09-16T19:36:48Z</updated><published>2026-09-14T10:55:13Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../../pages/www.france24.com/en/africa/20260914-nigeria-s-dangote-refinery-launches-continent-s-biggest-ipo--ce4a59a9b03f.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="http://www.france24.com/en/africa/20260914-nigeria-s-dangote-refinery-launches-continent-s-biggest-ipo" /><summary type="html">Nigerian billionaire Aliko Dangote on Monday launched what is slated to be Africa's biggest initial public offering (IPO), aiming to raise $1.6 billion in part to expand his mega oil refinery into the world's largest.</summary><category term="Africa" /></entry><entry><title>Children die in a school fire in Bukavu</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:725a1beb1f0846ed6066a1b8f582e7dbf77c648cd8e144f033b158eccdba3c56</id><updated>2026-09-16T19:36:48Z</updated><published>2026-09-11T20:18:17Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../../pages/www.france24.com/en/tv-shows/eye-on-africa/20260911-children-die-in-a-school-fire-in-bukavu--f4db6b01d2b9.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="http://www.france24.com/en/tv-shows/eye-on-africa/20260911-children-die-in-a-school-fire-in-bukavu" /><summary type="html">In this edition: children have died after a school fire broke out in the M23 rebel occupied city of Bukavu, Democratic Republic of Congo. Also, Senegal looks to the skies as it seeks to develop its home-grown satellite industry. And finally, we wonder about the future of African cities. How will they cope with the population boom and climate crisis? Some at the Kenya architecture biennale in Nairobi tried to answer these questions.</summary><category term="Eye on Africa" /></entry><entry><title>Ugandan royal succession scandal</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:6e374fb5b812ad82fe50d959ada85832814d52fe832a1ddcdcc99bde31998810</id><updated>2026-09-16T19:36:48Z</updated><published>2026-09-10T20:20:01Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../../pages/www.france24.com/en/ugandan-royal-succession-scandal--dc969309a6f8.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="http://www.france24.com/en/ugandan-royal-succession-scandal" /><summary type="html">In tonight's edition, a dramatic struggle for control of a throne in Uganda. Also, Rwanda shut down 101 alcoholic beverage factories last month and declared a public health crisis after at least 50 people were killed by contaminated illegal supplies. And Ethiopia celebrates its new year.</summary><category term="Eye on Africa" /></entry><entry><title>'Infantino is the one who has brought the most to Africa' says Samuel Eto'o</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:e688fa32aab37305b95b8afbb2328620af7d90f156d2960070644304e18aa20b</id><updated>2026-09-16T19:36:48Z</updated><published>2026-09-10T14:31:49Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../../pages/www.france24.com/en/tv-shows/t_te-_-t_te/20260910-infantino-is-the-one-who-has-brought-the-most-to-africa-says-samuel-eto-o--38a56194e182.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="http://www.france24.com/en/tv-shows/t%C3%AAte-%C3%A0-t%C3%AAte/20260910-infantino-is-the-one-who-has-brought-the-most-to-africa-says-samuel-eto-o" /><summary type="html">In an interview with FRANCE 24, former football star and current president of the Cameroonian Football Federation Samuel Eto'o spoke about the recent backlash towards FIFA president Gianni Infantino and confirmed he will support him for re-election because of "everything he has brought to African football", adding that the African federations will defend what seems "right to us given the challenges that we face in our own federations".</summary><category term="Tête à tête" /></entry><entry><title>Algeria cuts diplomatic ties with United Arab Emirates over 'provocative and hostile actions'</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:37eeec9672f2263dbd1520ca79f854fbd758bed0e760d4c643fc57e820db2ad5</id><updated>2026-09-16T19:36:48Z</updated><published>2026-09-10T11:50:14Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../../pages/www.france24.com/en/africa/20260910-algeria-cuts-diplomatic-ties-with-united-arab-emirates-over-provocative-and-hostile-actions--dd9c4cc89c40.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="http://www.france24.com/en/africa/20260910-algeria-cuts-diplomatic-ties-with-united-arab-emirates-over-provocative-and-hostile-actions" /><summary type="html">Algeria is severing diplomatic ties with the United Arab Emirates, state media reported Thursday. Algerian President ‌Abdelmadjid Tebboune has in the past accused the Gulf state of trying to meddle in Algeria's internal affairs.</summary><category term="Africa" /></entry><entry><title>Aid cuts force clinic closures across Sudan</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:d6e5e77d1a4ed73c56c3287c89e79fd1c3655baf2191ec0981793e6a4c0a90fc</id><updated>2026-09-16T19:36:48Z</updated><published>2026-09-09T20:32:55Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../../pages/www.france24.com/en/aid-cuts-force-clinic-closures-across-sudan--1bc1aa71732f.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="http://www.france24.com/en/aid-cuts-force-clinic-closures-across-sudan" /><summary type="html">In tonight's edition: aid groups are warning that funding cuts are forcing dozens of health clinics in Sudan to close. Also, an AI and Africa conference has kicked off in South Korea's capital. Finally, we take a look at the Rwanda Space Agency as Paris hosts its very first international space summit.</summary><category term="Eye on Africa" /></entry><entry><title>JNIM jihadists step up incursions into northern Benin</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:8cb7c2803851408c08a106650d367d9be0d4881198f5080c3113f75f24e4922b</id><updated>2026-09-16T19:36:48Z</updated><published>2026-09-09T10:00:18Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../../pages/www.france24.com/en/tv-shows/the-observers/20260909-jnim-jihadists-step-up-incursions-northern-benin--c61528c3ec0c.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="http://www.france24.com/en/tv-shows/the-observers/20260909-jnim-jihadists-step-up-incursions-northern-benin" /><summary type="html">Al Qaeda affiliate JNIM has been intensifying its activities in Benin since 2022. The country serves as a transit corridor between the areas of operation of the group, which is well established in Mali, Niger and Burkina Faso. Two nature reserves have become logistical hubs for the jihadist fighters. Benin's army is striving to hold the line.</summary><category term="The Observers" /></entry><entry><title>Kenya cracks down on foreign traders</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:cddc82fd6982e20aeb251a05a222b2134e46372ea5a7500855e77c9133d96b0b</id><updated>2026-09-16T19:36:48Z</updated><published>2026-09-08T20:29:13Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../../pages/www.france24.com/en/kenya-cracks-down-on-foreign-traders--c31b8c5d613f.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="http://www.france24.com/en/kenya-cracks-down-on-foreign-traders" /><summary type="html">In tonight's edition, Hundreds of Burundians flocked to ⁠their embassy in ​Nairobi to get travel documents to return home. Also, in his first speech to the National assembly, Senegal’s Prime Minister Ahmadou Al Aminou Lo slams the rise of xenophobic rhetoric on the continent. Also, FRANCE 24's Georja Calvin Smith speaks to the founder of an AI surgical non-profit about the work being done in Tanzania to extend the reach of the country's 27 overburdened neurosurgeons.</summary><category term="Eye on Africa" /></entry><entry><title>Egyptian influencer faces death penalty over drug charges</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:f1cf797285b00ea8e50cc64249629787c2a1d3e29d652a71b72839d47ab9d275</id><updated>2026-09-16T19:36:48Z</updated><published>2026-09-07T20:27:19Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../../pages/www.france24.com/en/egyptian-influencer-s-death-penalty-over-drug-charges--5338612a10b4.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="http://www.france24.com/en/egyptian-influencer-s-death-penalty-over-drug-charges" /><summary type="html">In tonight's edition: An Egyptian TV presenter is among a dozen people a court has sentenced to death on drug charges. But first: a South African high court rejects a former defence minister's bid to have a corruption case against her dropped. Plus the very first Pan-African Architecture Biennale opened its doors on Monday in the Kenyan capital Nairobi.</summary><category term="Eye on Africa" /></entry><entry><title>Tunisians tell the story of their summer of shortages</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:37fe91ed81452a0eb4daf7b9cebd8e5a0a0c4841c7a3e7b261016d08698f6529</id><updated>2026-09-16T19:36:48Z</updated><published>2026-09-07T18:50:16Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../../pages/www.france24.com/en/africa/20260907-tunisians-tell-story-their-summer-shortages--617d7548111f.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="http://www.france24.com/en/africa/20260907-tunisians-tell-story-their-summer-shortages" /><summary type="html">Frequent water and power cuts, long queues for bottled water, poultry farms destroyed by the heat: these have been the scenes across Tunisia this summer as millions of people were affected by shortages in electricity and water. Our Observers say this is the result of years of underinvestment in infrastructure while the Tunisian president, for his part, blames "deliberate and planned acts of sabotage”.</summary><category term="Africa" /></entry><entry><title>Egyptian TV presenter Sarah Khalifa sentenced to death in drug case</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:0ae6eae6f844be900e188320cba69903cd3490b2c0c72300e2a49b9aae7ea883</id><updated>2026-09-16T19:36:48Z</updated><published>2026-09-06T13:52:42Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../../pages/www.france24.com/en/africa/20260906-egyptian-tv-presenter-sarah-khalifa-sentenced-to-death-in-drug-case--a5ffad89f8f8.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="http://www.france24.com/en/africa/20260906-egyptian-tv-presenter-sarah-khalifa-sentenced-to-death-in-drug-case" /><summary type="html">Sarah Khalifa, a well-known Egyptian TV presenter, has been sentenced to death for her involvement in a drug ring, state media reported over the weekend. Eleven others were also convicted in the case and handed a death sentence. Khalifa is best known for her program “Mission Impossible”.</summary><category term="Africa" /></entry><entry><title>Uber withdraws from Nigeria and Uganda</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:5555dbad04c002b987b88a0c2c5d9e5ab8c1e9b93b9510cb82b94c2fab639e23</id><updated>2026-09-16T19:36:48Z</updated><published>2026-09-04T20:39:08Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../../pages/www.france24.com/en/uber-withdraws-from-nigeria--7fddf4430022.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="http://www.france24.com/en/uber-withdraws-from-nigeria" /><summary type="html">In tonight's programme: Uber is pulling out of Nigeria and Uganda, leaving thousands of drivers scrambling for new sources of income. But first: a UN report says foreign fighters and support networks are fuelling Sudan's war and worsening violence against civilians. Finally, we discuss the African Diaspora International Film Festival with co-director Diarah N'daw-Spech and Laurence Cole, co-producer of the Nigerian film "Shadow of Greed".</summary><category term="Eye on Africa" /></entry><entry><title>'Moroccans are desperate: Years of political crackdown and lack of economic opportunities'</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:ce6435a13a2cb73205d027ddfbbc71e9b540da596353ce8d11ce9e617a558c88</id><updated>2026-09-16T19:36:48Z</updated><published>2026-09-04T12:30:37Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../../pages/www.france24.com/en/tv-shows/spotlight/20260904-moroccans-are-desperate-years-of-political-crackdown-and-lack-of-economic-opportunities--0197ef1ebe1f.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="http://www.france24.com/en/tv-shows/spotlight/20260904-moroccans-are-desperate-years-of-political-crackdown-and-lack-of-economic-opportunities" /><summary type="html">François Picard is pleased to welcome Aida Alami, New York Times World contributor. Drawing on her own field reporting at the Moroccan border, Alami challenges attempts to explain the mass crossing to the Spanish enclave of Ceuta primarily through a theory of deliberate state orchestration. While acknowledging both Morocco's history of leveraging migration in its relations with Spain and the possibility that security forces made tactical decisions on the day, Alami insists on distinguishing evidence from inference.</summary><category term="Spotlight" /></entry><entry><title>Mass burial reveals hidden toll of Algeria’s deadly ‘Black Wednesday’ wildfires</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:f4dee0e90303c601b4f0d4e24a9afd16cfdb6b83a1c43cbfe59e80abee8c1dd7</id><updated>2026-09-16T19:36:48Z</updated><published>2026-09-04T11:33:04Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../../pages/www.france24.com/en/africa/20260904-mass-burial-algeria-black-wednesday-deadly-wildfires--e2ca45613885.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="http://www.france24.com/en/africa/20260904-mass-burial-algeria-black-wednesday-deadly-wildfires" /><summary type="html">Wildfires ripped across northeastern Algeria in late August. Photos and videos showing the mass burial of several dozen people have raised questions about the real death toll of the fires as Algerian authorities haven’t shared new numbers since August 26. A number of Moroccan social media accounts have also been sharing disinformation about the fires.</summary><category term="Africa" /></entry><entry><title>'Pragmatic international relations': Sánchez seeks to avoid 'further conflict with Rabat'</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:4d5afa2a8d2fbd2ea3e2529c6fd82282726506b1f618d07e8e25bd67395ecf97</id><updated>2026-09-16T19:36:48Z</updated><published>2026-09-04T05:22:52Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../../pages/www.france24.com/en/video/20260904-pragmatic-international-relations-s_nchez-seeks-to-avoid-further-conflict-with-rabat--c4a5b9cb15c4.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="http://www.france24.com/en/video/20260904-pragmatic-international-relations-s%C3%A1nchez-seeks-to-avoid-further-conflict-with-rabat" /><summary type="html">Delano D'Souza is pleased to welcome Alana Moceri, International Relations Professor at IE School of Politics, Economics and Global Affairs. The migrant crossings at Ceuta have become both a domestic and international political lightning rod. At home, Pedro Sánchez faces opponents who can cast migration in existential terms, with Spain's far right amplifying the rhetoric of "invasion." Internationally, the crisis exposes the geopolitical leverage inherent in control of the border. Ceuta thus emerges as more than a migration crisis: the border becomes at once an instrument of geopolitical leverage, a weapon in Spain's domestic political contest, and a battleground for competing narratives and disinformation.</summary></entry><entry><title>Tunisia top court upholds long sentences in mass trial of opposition figures</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:607595261303c65bc016d27a375d74a2902cc4ebeb2c0fd04ce0eb9efbcc0916</id><updated>2026-09-16T19:36:48Z</updated><published>2026-09-03T22:17:25Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../../pages/www.france24.com/en/africa/20260903-tunisia-court-upholds-prison-sentences-of-up-to-45-years-for-opposition-figures--25780c534aec.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="http://www.france24.com/en/africa/20260903-tunisia-court-upholds-prison-sentences-of-up-to-45-years-for-opposition-figures" /><summary type="html">Tunisia's top court on Thursday upheld prison sentences of up to 45 years for opposition leaders, lawyers and businessmen convicted in a major conspiracy case, in a ruling opponents said further entrenches President Kais Saied's authoritarian rule.</summary><category term="Africa" /></entry><entry><title>Shady surgeons and Nigeria organ harvesting</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:ba28adc2084bfa6377f30c13bca9bab68ae47939922ec28147b6571ea33fbd4d</id><updated>2026-09-16T19:36:48Z</updated><published>2026-09-03T20:15:15Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../../pages/www.france24.com/en/shady-surgeons-and-nigeria-organ-harvesting--d87a92aad99d.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="http://www.france24.com/en/shady-surgeons-and-nigeria-organ-harvesting" /><summary type="html">In tonight's edition: Legal loopholes and gaps about specialised medicine may be driving illegal organ harvesting in Nigeria. Also, South Sudan may be moving closer to delivering on elections scheduled for December. Finally, on the islands of São Tomé and Príncipe, six colonial-era plantation estates have just been added to the UNESCO World Heritage List.</summary><category term="Eye on Africa" /></entry><entry><title>Tunisia water crisis deepens as temperatures approach 50°C</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:11666dfbe8e9059765e60267d682f2f668cc16e443639c54ed1718a3093be443</id><updated>2026-09-16T19:36:48Z</updated><published>2026-09-03T13:52:23Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../../pages/www.france24.com/en/video/20260903-tunisia-water-crisis-deepens-as-temperatures-approach-50_c--25ad983d7cc3.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="http://www.france24.com/en/video/20260903-tunisia-water-crisis-deepens-as-temperatures-approach-50%C2%B0c" /><summary type="html">In Tunisia, powerful heatwaves are driving power cuts and water shortage. While the country has been dealing with structural water scarcity and recurring drought for years, the current crisis is fuelling anger against the government, even as authorities attempt to coordinate water distribution efforts to quell the crisis.</summary></entry><entry><title>Kenyan editor found safe after abduction</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:fcfeacc90bcdf8035ccbe41bae25f96f99b45b0d1db0c4a853cc44e5aa2c17a3</id><updated>2026-09-16T19:36:48Z</updated><published>2026-09-02T20:15:44Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../../pages/www.france24.com/en/kenyan-editor-found-safe-after-abduction--7cd7d68f36ec.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="http://www.france24.com/en/kenyan-editor-found-safe-after-abduction" /><summary type="html">In tonight's edition: Media rights groups in Kenya call for an investigation after a top journalist is abducted overnight by heavily armed men. Also, trials for three potential vaccines against the Bundibugyo strain of Ebola are due to start over the next couple of months. Plus Senegalese consumers who are frustrated by the rising cost of living have launched a new campaign.</summary><category term="Eye on Africa" /></entry><entry><title>Spanish report flags Moroccan police's 'total permissiveness' in Ceuta migrant rush</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:f1b78abbb06a4251d2283a8237e3f87f3bbfa61c3d44baf5ad2e736daa47a788</id><updated>2026-09-16T19:36:48Z</updated><published>2026-09-02T19:20:07Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../../pages/www.france24.com/en/africa/20260902-spanish-report-flags-moroccan-police-s-total-permissiveness-in-ceuta-migrant-rush--02ef226c47f6.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="http://www.france24.com/en/africa/20260902-spanish-report-flags-moroccan-police-s-total-permissiveness-in-ceuta-migrant-rush" /><summary type="html">Moroccan security forces displayed "total permissiveness" during last month's massive wave of migrants who crossed into the Spanish North African territory of Ceuta, according to a Spanish police report seen by a French news agency on Wednesday.</summary><category term="Africa" /></entry><entry><title>IMF reaches historical loan agreement with Senegal</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:71b9f3ed7793a6b88c701b14afff3568d74fd88702b119a4d626199c699df1d2</id><updated>2026-09-16T19:36:48Z</updated><published>2026-09-02T12:51:27Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../../pages/www.france24.com/en/imf-reaches-historical-loan-agreement-with-senegal--2eb64631acbe.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="http://www.france24.com/en/imf-reaches-historical-loan-agreement-with-senegal" /><summary type="html">The IMF and Senegal have agreed on a new 2.2 billion-dollar loan programme to support financial reforms, a relief for the country after a huge amount of unreported debt recently came to light. The president has accused his predecessor of hiding the true state of the country's imbalanced budget deficit.</summary></entry><entry><title>Kenya shifts to electric cars as fuel prices soar 50%</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:8167e054380aea2acab39729415349ed639cf68033fef335498ae665035b43c2</id><updated>2026-09-16T19:36:48Z</updated><published>2026-09-02T11:19:05Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../../pages/www.france24.com/en/video/20260902-kenya-shifts-to-electric-cars-as-fuel-prices-soar-50--214cc24f9987.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="http://www.france24.com/en/video/20260902-kenya-shifts-to-electric-cars-as-fuel-prices-soar-50" /><summary type="html">In Kenya, locally made eletric vehicles are gaining traction as fuel prices rise. As more and more Kenyans consider going electric, local firms are stepping up to assemble EV's domestically.</summary></entry><entry><title>Illegal mining ravages Johannesburg neighbourhoods</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:641a47ed265d53dfdef5b4b13c3adf9331b6f11f4434bb38e751a759ca098dfa</id><updated>2026-09-16T19:36:48Z</updated><published>2026-09-02T11:14:09Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../../pages/www.france24.com/en/video/20260902-illegal-mining-ravages-johannesburg-neighbourhoods--a75e47943773.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="http://www.france24.com/en/video/20260902-illegal-mining-ravages-johannesburg-neighbourhoods" /><summary type="html">Some houses in one Johannesburg neighbourhood are in danger of collapse because of illegal mining. Known as Zama Zamas, around 34,000 illegal miners are thought to be working in South Africa, in the hope of finding gold and chromium. The mines they dig hollow out the ground, which causes buildings to collapse as well as frequent power outages.</summary></entry><entry><title>Military buildup seen in Ethiopia near Sudanese border</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:47abcaa39598f4e99d0611e4d7b272588622deb7c72b438b3058c1b55d32b3b5</id><updated>2026-09-16T19:36:48Z</updated><published>2026-09-02T10:57:17Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../../pages/www.france24.com/en/video/20260902-military-buildup-seen-in-ethiopia-near-sudanese-border--3f84da8e9b1a.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="http://www.france24.com/en/video/20260902-military-buildup-seen-in-ethiopia-near-sudanese-border" /><summary type="html">A major military buildup has been observed on the Ethiopian side of the Sudanese border. It comes amid intensified fighting between the Sudanese armed forces and the Rapid Support Forces (RSF). Sudan and Ethiopia have both accused each other of supporting rebels within their borders.</summary></entry><entry><title>South Africans missing in Nepal's glacial flood</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:a5188de0934d2ca64aa00c88f7bb4645e91f31d68343d10a07fb651f09819736</id><updated>2026-09-16T19:36:48Z</updated><published>2026-09-01T20:35:30Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../../pages/www.france24.com/en/south-africans-missing-in-nepal-s-glacial-flood--856bd6e9f51b.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="http://www.france24.com/en/south-africans-missing-in-nepal-s-glacial-flood" /><summary type="html">In tonight's edition: Africa Disaster Relief is looking for six South Africans who went missing in the deadly floods on the border between Nepal and China. Also, South African illegal miners are undermining their own communities. Finally, rising fuel prices are pushing Kenyans towards electric vehicles.</summary><category term="Eye on Africa" /></entry></feed>
